IDEA 1: Predictive Value Zeroing — From cached penultimate student tokens and final-block attention, reconstruct each patch’s CLS contribution \(c_i=\sum_h A^h_{\mathrm{CLS},i}W_O^hv_i^h\); let \(u\) be CLS after the attention residual.
Replay only the CLS MLP, final normalization and classifier: score \(s_i=\mathrm{KL}(p(u)\Vert p(u-c_i))\), using identical stochastic masks across interventions; keep top-K original patches. This explains which value messages affect species predictions, without coordinates or teacher access.  
— basis: Mohebbi et al., [*Quantifying Context Mixing in Transformers*, EACL 2023](https://aclanthology.org/2023.eacl-main.245/): Value Zeroing improved linguistic rationale, probing and faithfulness evaluations; predictive KL is the proposed adaptation, with CUB gains untested.  
— closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [Adaptive Token Sampling, ECCV 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.15667) combines attention with value magnitude; this measures the actual downstream prediction change, including head cancellation and the nonlinear CLS MLP.  
— extra FLOPs: Approximately \(20Nd^2+2NdC\), with \(N=196,C=200\): **0.16 GFLOPs/image at \(d=192\)** or **0.61 at \(d=384\)**, including value/output projections and 196 CLS-only counterfactuals; no backward pass.

IDEA 2: D-optimal Contrastive Attribution — At penultimate student patch states \(h_i\), compute four signed replacement attributions \(g_{ij}=(h_i-\bar h)^\top\nabla_{h_i}(z_c-z_{r_j})\), where \(\bar h\) is the image’s patch-state mean, \(c\) the predicted class and \(r_j\) its four highest-scoring alternatives; use `autograd.grad`, retaining the training graph.
Starting with \(S=\varnothing\), repeatedly select the unchosen patch maximizing \(\log(1+g_i^\top M_S^{-1}g_i)\), where \(M_S=\lambda I+\sum_{t\in S}g_tg_t^\top\) and \(\lambda=\operatorname{mean}_i\|g_i\|^2/4+10^{-8}\), until K patches remain: a local explanation covering complementary class distinctions, without spatial coordinates.  
— inspired by: Joshi and Boyd, [*Sensor Selection via Convex Optimization*, IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing, 2009](https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/papers/sensor_selection.html).  
— why it transfers: Its determinant criterion rewards measurements that resolve different uncertainty directions; treating signed class-margin attributions as measurements could preserve complementary bird-identification evidence. The paper supports sensor-estimation efficiency; transfer to teacher-token selection remains a hypothesis, and the greedy rule is an adaptation.  
— queries tried: experimental design sensor selection log determinant Fisher information complementary measurements; contrastive explanations fine grained classification discriminative evidence similar classes; batch active learning gradient embeddings diversity BADGE ICLR 2020; sparse sensor placement optimal reconstruction pivoted QR data driven measurements; coreset selection gradient matching orthogonal matching pursuit GradMatch; information bottleneck minimal sufficient explanations feature subsets neural predictions  
— closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [CDPruner, NeurIPS 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10967) uses determinantal selection over instruction-conditioned feature similarity; this uses signed student class-margin attribution vectors, making complementarity predictive rather than merely representational.  
— extra FLOPs: Four last-block input-gradient passes, roughly **0.8–1.6 GFLOPs/image at \(d=192\)**, plus negligible \(O(196K\,4^2)\) selection; gradients must be billed. Both proposals require paired same-K wins over maskedkd, rollout, tome, grid, center and random, and mean accuracy within the supplied Full-KD tolerance—neither has demonstrated those wins.